{"date": "2026-10-09", "post_id": 1424821, "title": "Stickney Crater", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-9-stickney-crater/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Stickney Crater, the largest crater on the martian moon Phobos, is named for <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angeline_Stickney\">Chloe Angeline Stickney</a> Hall, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/anastronomerswi03hallgoog\">mathematician and</a> wife of astronomer Asaph Hall. Asaph Hall discovered both the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=201030\">Red Planet's moons</a> in 1877. Over 9 kilometers across, Stickney is nearly half the diameter of Phobos itself, so large that the impact that blasted out the crater likely came close to shattering the tiny moon. This <a href=\"http://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/phobos.php\">enhanced-color image</a> of Stickney and surroundings was recorded by the HiRISE camera onboard the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter as it passed within some six thousand kilometers <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=170721\">of Phobos</a> in March of 2008. Even though the surface gravity of <a href=\"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/moons/mars-moons/phobos/in-depth/\">asteroid-like Phobos</a> is less than 1/1000th Earth's gravity, streaks suggest loose material slid down inside the crater walls over time. Light bluish regions near the crater's rim could indicate a relatively freshly exposed surface. The origin of the curious grooves along the surface is mysterious but may be related to tidal stresses experienced by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220703\">close-orbiting</a> Phobos or <a href=\"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016GeoRL..4310595B\">the crater-forming impact</a> itself.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>farside", "credit": "<a href=\"https://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/\">HiRISE</a>, <a href=\"https://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/mro/\">MRO</a>, <a href=\"https://www.lpl.arizona.edu/\">LPL (U. Arizona)</a>, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/\">HiRISE</a>, <a href=\"https://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/mro/\">MRO</a>, <a href=\"https://www.lpl.arizona.edu/\">LPL (U. Arizona)</a>, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>", "alt": "A color enhanced close-up image of Stickney Crater on Phobos.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-9-stickney-crater/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg?w=1836&h=1362&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 9 - Stickney Crater</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-9-stickney-crater/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Stickney Crater, the largest crater on the martian moon Phobos, is named for Chloe Angeline Stickney Hall, mathematician and wife of astronomer Asaph Hall. Asaph Hall discovered both the Red Planet&#039;s moons in 1877. Over 9 kilometers across, Stickney is nearly half the diameter of Phobos itself, so large that the impact that blasted out the crater likely came close to shattering the tiny moon. This enhanced-color image of Stickney and surroundings was recorded by the HiRISE camera onboard the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter as it passed within some six thousand kilometers of Phobos in March of 2008. Even though the surface gravity of asteroid-like Phobos is less than 1/1000th Earth&#039;s gravity, streaks suggest loose material slid down inside the crater walls over time. Light bluish regions near the crater&#039;s rim could indicate a relatively freshly exposed surface. The origin of the curious grooves along the surface is mysterious but may be related to tidal stresses experienced by close-orbiting Phobos or the crater-forming impact itself.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: farside\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"stickney crater\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Stickney Crater\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-9-stickney-crater/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg?w=1836&#038;h=1362&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg?w=1836&#038;h=1362&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"A color enhanced close-up image of Stickney Crater on Phobos.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-09T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-07T09:22:24-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Stickney Crater\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg?w=1836&#038;h=1362&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 9<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/PSP_007769_9010_IRB_Stickney.jpg\" alt=\"A color enhanced close-up image of Stickney Crater on Phobos.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Stickney Crater </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/\">HiRISE</a>, <a href=\"https://mars.jpl.nasa.gov/mro/\">MRO</a>, <a href=\"https://www.lpl.arizona.edu/\">LPL (U. Arizona)</a>, <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Stickney Crater, the largest crater on the martian moon Phobos, is named for <a href=\"http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angeline_Stickney\">Chloe Angeline Stickney</a> Hall, <a href=\"https://archive.org/details/anastronomerswi03hallgoog\">mathematician and</a> wife of astronomer Asaph Hall. Asaph Hall discovered both the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=201030\">Red Planet's moons</a> in 1877. Over 9 kilometers across, Stickney is nearly half the diameter of Phobos itself, so large that the impact that blasted out the crater likely came close to shattering the tiny moon. This <a href=\"http://hirise.lpl.arizona.edu/phobos.php\">enhanced-color image</a> of Stickney and surroundings was recorded by the HiRISE camera onboard the Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter as it passed within some six thousand kilometers <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=170721\">of Phobos</a> in March of 2008. Even though the surface gravity of <a href=\"https://solarsystem.nasa.gov/moons/mars-moons/phobos/in-depth/\">asteroid-like Phobos</a> is less than 1/1000th Earth's gravity, streaks suggest loose material slid down inside the crater walls over time. Light bluish regions near the crater's rim could indicate a relatively freshly exposed surface. The origin of the curious grooves along the surface is mysterious but may be related to tidal stresses experienced by <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/?date=220703\">close-orbiting</a> Phobos or <a href=\"http://adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2016GeoRL..4310595B\">the crater-forming impact</a> itself.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>farside\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-8-the-saturn-system-smorgasbord/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nJerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261009/html"}